WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:15.296
A neurone is a nerve cell. It carries electrical signals called nerve impulses. Here is a motor neurone. At one end is the cell body. Inside it is the nucleus. Short branches, called dendrites, come out of the cell body.

00:00:15.850 --> 00:00:37.674
From the cell body, one long fibre runs away: the axon. It carries impulses away from the cell body. A fatty layer, the myelin sheath, wraps around the axon. At the far end, the axon splits into small branches, the axon terminals, ending in synaptic knobs: small swellings that pass the impulse on to the next cell.

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:47.035
What does the myelin sheath do? It insulates the axon, like the plastic round a wire. This makes the impulses travel faster.

00:00:47.600 --> 00:01:00.080
Sensory and relay neurones have the same parts, in different places. In a sensory neurone the cell body sits part-way along the fibre. But for a drawing, the exam usually wants a motor neurone.

00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:09.695
To draw a neurone, keep it simple. Step one: a round cell body at one end, a nucleus inside it, and several short dendrites around it.

00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:17.525
Step two: one long axon. Make it many times longer than the cell body is wide. End it with a few small branches.

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:26.925
Step three: draw the myelin sheath as blocks along the axon. Step four: add each label with a ruled line that touches the exact part.

00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:38.653
Now a real exam question. It asks you to draw a diagram of a motor neurone, and to label the nucleus, the axon and the myelin sheath. Four marks.

00:01:39.250 --> 00:01:47.165
Mark one is for the shape. A round cell body at one end with dendrites. Then a long axon ending in small branches.

00:01:47.750 --> 00:01:56.859
Now the three labels, each with a ruled line. The nucleus line touches the dot in the cell body. The axon line touches the long fibre.

00:01:57.450 --> 00:02:07.285
Draw the myelin sheath as blocks along the axon, and label one block. Four marks. This answer is by olevels.com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:17.211
Common mistakes: a nucleus drawn on the axon, an axon no longer than the cell body, and label lines that stop short of the part.

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:26.269
When you compare two neurones, name the feature. Count the branches, look at their length, and count the synaptic knobs. Do not just write smaller.

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:40.179
Your turn. Neurone X is a motor neurone. Neurone Y is a sensory neurone. Give two differences in structure between X and Y. Name the feature each time. Pause here and work it out.

00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:59.732
One: in X, the cell body is at one end of the neurone. In Y, the cell body is part-way along the fibre, on a short side branch. Two: X has dendrites around its cell body. Y has no dendrites on its cell body. Each difference names a feature and compares both neurones.

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:10.988
So, every time: cell body at one end with a nucleus and dendrites. A long axon with myelin blocks and small branches. Ruled labels that touch the parts.
